From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Agent <claude-agent@invalid>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 20:14:57 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] native: Composite surfaces and cursors whose highlights are
 mapped

Direct scanout bypasses the blending state, so a surface whose
rendering would map highlights toward the output's target must not be
scanned out: the panel would receive the unmapped signal. The scanout
decision only asked whether the surface's color state needs any
conversion to the output's, which says nothing about the mapping: the
output color state declares no target.

Factor the color-state part of the decision into
meta_compositor_view_native_color_state_prevents_scanout(), exported for
tests: a surface is composited if converting it to the output state is
not empty, or if clutter_color_transform_requires_highlight_mapping()
holds from the surface's color state to the view's blending state, the
same predicate, with the same pair of states, by which the renderer
adds the mapping. So direct scanout is refused exactly when the mapping
applies to a surface in the output's encoding. Content declaring the
target as its own peak, or mastered below it, is still scanned out
when its encoding matches.

The hardware cursor plane bypasses the blending state too: the cursor
sprite is converted straight to the output color state. When the same
predicate holds from the cursor's color state to the view's blending
state (for example SDR white with a reference luminance above the
declared target peak), the view uses no hardware cursor and the cursor
is drawn in the stage, mapped like everything else; the sprite is
marked invalid so that it is realized again once mapping no longer
applies.

The decision is re-evaluated whenever the view's blending or output
color state changes: both notifications invalidate the view's hardware
cursor and mark the view as needing a cursor update, and the cursor
renderer's prepare_frame, run for every frame of the view, forces that
update on the first frame after the change, rather than waiting for the
cursor to move or change. The update clears the mark, so no frame
afterwards pays for it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DLZiLBYev72J1s4D37L7Bi
